/** All files concatenated — the pre-toggle fallback. */
export const BRIGHTLOCAL_RULES = "BrightLocal house rules (from the design system's AI_USAGE.md):\n- dataHook is REQUIRED on every user-interactive component root (Button, Input, Select, Dialog, Tabs, …) and optional on structural ones. It renders as a data-hook attribute. Naming: kebab-case {context}-{componentType} — \"settings-save-button\", \"login-email-input\"; lists include the item id (\"user-{id}-row\"). Never generic names like \"test-button\".\n- Styling: semantic color tokens first (primary, secondary, muted, destructive, …); fall back to Tailwind color utilities (text-blue-600) only when no token fits; NEVER raw hex/rgb values.\n- Use DS components, not raw HTML: <Button> not <button>, <Input> not <input>, Typography for text.\n- Compose compound components from their provided sub-exports; don't rebuild patterns from primitives.\n- Transitions: always name the property (transition-colors, transition-opacity); never transition-all.\n- Focus styles: focus-visible: prefix only; never bare focus:.\n- Icons come from @brightlocal/icons at their default 16px — no size/strokeWidth overrides.\n- Illustrations come from @brightlocal/illustrations (named PascalCase exports — RobotAiA, MapPinLocation, …; see the Illustration sidecar). ALWAYS pass a size (default 250 is a poster); decorative uses get aria-hidden. They carry a fixed brand palette — pick the `variant` for the surface, never tint via CSS.\n- Forms: the canonical pattern is Field > FieldLabel + control + FieldDescription + FieldError.\n- Branding: app chrome (sidebar headers, top bars) uses the <Logo /> component — the BrightLocal mark — not hand-rolled initial tiles or text logos.\n- NEVER restyle a DS component's own chrome with utility classes. No border/background/rounded/padding/state overrides on TabsTrigger, TabsList, Button, Input, Card internals, etc. — render them BARE and let the design system paint them (e.g. <TabsTrigger value=\"overview\" dataHook=\"tab-overview\">Overview</TabsTrigger>, nothing more). Utility classes are for LAYOUT AROUND components only: spacing, width, grid/flex placement. If a component looks wrong bare, the fix is a variant or size prop, never className surgery.\n- No celebration effects (canvas-confetti) in BrightLocal screens.\n- Charts: always wrap in a container with an explicit fixed height (h-64, h-80) — never height=\"100%\" inside an unsized parent (recharts logs width(-1) and renders nothing).\n\nBrightLocal product map — how to interpret lazy vertical prompts.\nWhen a request names a product vertical (\"the hub page for Citations\", \"rankings page\", \"reviews dashboard\"), do NOT invent navigation: use the standard app shell (SidebarProvider > GlobalLayout > GlobalLayoutSidebar > Sidebar with Header/Content/Footer, footer = SidebarAccountDropdown), set isActive on that vertical's nav item, and title the page header for the vertical. Location nav items, in order: Location Summary, AI Insights, Rankings, Local Search Grid, Citations, GBP, Reputation & Reviews.\nResponsive shell: the sidebar hides below lg (the live platform's behaviour). Every app-shell screen must include a mobile top bar as the FIRST child of GlobalLayoutContent: <header className=\"flex items-center gap-1 px-1 py-1 lg:hidden\"><SidebarTrigger dataHook=\"mobile-sidebar-trigger\" className=\"size-11\"><Menu className=\"size-5\" /></SidebarTrigger><Logo className=\"h-6\" dataHook=\"mobile-logo\" /></header>. Keep header padding minimal (px-1): the 44px trigger already carries ~12px internal inset, so the glyph lands ~16px from the screen edge like the live platform — no extra left padding/margin on the trigger itself. The Menu (three-line hamburger, from @brightlocal/icons) matches the live platform; the size-11 (44px) className is the minimum touch target and the size-5 glyph matches the sidebar nav icons. No border-b — the live platform's mobile bar has no dividing line. SidebarTrigger opens the design system's built-in mobile sheet — never hand-roll a drawer.\nDemo persona: the logged-in user in every screen is Andy Smith (andy@acmecorp.com, avatar initials AS) — use exactly this in the SidebarAccountDropdown and anywhere a signed-in user appears.\nSidebar nav icons: lucide icons from @brightlocal/icons at 20px (className=\"size-5\", default strokeWidth) — inside sidebar navigation this overrides the general \"16px\" icon rule. (Deliberate deviation: the live platform ships 24px/strokeWidth 1.33 here, which contradicts its own docs and reads oversized; 20px is our curated middle.) Icon pairings: Your Locations=House, Location Summary=LayoutDashboard, AI Insights=Sparkles, Rankings=TrendingUp, Local Search Grid=Grid3x3, Citations=Link, GBP=Store, Reputation & Reviews=Star, Location Manager=Building, White-label=Globe.\nHub-page conventions per vertical (a hub = score/stat summary row + one primary work surface + one CTA):\n- Citations — citations-found score (e.g. 53/60), listings sync status, directory table (directory name, listing status, NAP accuracy), CTA \"Build Citations\".\n- Rankings — average ranking position, keywords in top 3, search visibility %, tracked-keywords table, CTA \"Run New Audit\".\n- Local Search Grid — geo-grid map as the primary surface, keyword selector, competitor comparison beside it.\n- GBP — profile-completeness score, field-level audit checklist, sync status.\n- Reputation & Reviews — average rating, review velocity, per-site breakdown (Google, Facebook, Yelp), reply queue.\n- Location Summary — the overview dashboard: visibility score, rating, citations and actions-required stat cards.\n\nLocal-SEO glossary — use these terms correctly in generated copy:\n- GBP = Google Business Profile (formerly GMB / Google My Business).\n- NAP = Name, Address, Phone — consistency across directories is the core citations quality metric.\n- Citation = any online mention of a business's NAP (directories, apps, map services).\n- SERP = search engine results page; the \"local pack\" is the 3-result map unit at the top.\n- Geo-grid = a map grid of ranking positions sampled at points around a location.\n- SoLV = Share of Local Voice — % of grid points where the business ranks top 3.\n- Review velocity = the rate of new reviews over time.\nIn UI copy, expand an acronym on first use (\"Google Business Profile (GBP)\"), then use the acronym.\n\n@brightlocal/proposal — the shared proposal module (USE IT BY DEFAULT):\n\n- AI INSIGHTS WORK: the working doc is registries/brightlocal/AI-INSIGHTS.md — the live-product captures (-1 Studios verbatim), the full aiInsights/foundation/visibility shape, locked decisions (text-varies-numbers-don't, arithmetic Location Score, per-action deep links, hub-and-spoke), the old↔new label map, screen ids, known gaps and next steps. Read it BEFORE touching AI Insights data, screens, or copy.\n\n- For ANY hub, dashboard, or full-page screen with the app sidebar, import the shell and chrome from \"@brightlocal/proposal\" instead of composing GlobalLayout/Sidebar by hand or copying shell code into the screen:\n  import { AppLayoutShell, ProposalSidebar, PageHeader, StatCard, HubStatCard, HubHeroCard, useProposalData } from \"@brightlocal/proposal\";\n- EVERY compact metric tile is a <StatCard> (label/value/delta/tone/icon/info; metricKey binds from data.metrics; level=\"nested\" for a stat row ON a bigger card). Never hand-roll Card + CardContent className=\"pt-6\" for stats — BL's Card already pads content and pt-6 double-pads the top.\n- The canonical page skeleton is:\n  <SidebarProvider defaultOpen>\n    <AppLayoutShell flush stickyHeader pinnedSidebar sidebarTone=\"white\"\n      sidebar={<ProposalSidebar />}\n      header={<PageHeader breadcrumbs={[…]} title=\"…\" meta={…} />}>\n      <GlobalLayoutContentBody>…page content…</GlobalLayoutContentBody>\n    </AppLayoutShell>\n  </SidebarProvider>\n  SidebarProvider and GlobalLayoutContentBody still come from \"@brightlocal/ui-components\".\n- AppLayoutShell knobs are LITERAL props (the inspector edits them): flush, stickyHeader, pinnedSidebar, sidebarTone (\"default\" | \"white\" | \"subtle\" | \"dark\" | \"brand\"), sidebarFrame (\"flush\" | \"floating\" | \"attached\"), sidebarShadow, pageLayers (\"default\" | \"raised\"), navDensity (\"compact\" | \"comfortable\" | \"expansive\"), contentMaxWidth, tweaker.\n- NAV DENSITY is variable-driven: the shell's scoped style consumes --gds-nav-font-size / --gds-nav-row-py / --gds-nav-sub-py / --gds-nav-sub-pl / --gds-nav-icon-size / --gds-nav-icon-stroke (compact defaults: 14px text, ~30px rows, subs left-aligned to the main label, 16px/stroke-1 icons). navDensity=\"comfortable\" re-points them (roomier rows, 20px/1.5 icons); \"expansive\" mirrors the LIVE product nav (~48px rows, 15px labels, 20px icons). Also a tweaker row (Alt+T). Set the variables directly for per-screen fine-tuning.\n- LOCKED VARIANT screens (A/B comparisons, hard-set client data): when the user asks for comparison screens or a screen pinned to specific data, hard-set the differing knobs as plain props AND set tweaker={false} so the screen is locked to its authored look (no Alt+T override layer). E.g. sidebarTone=\"brand\" tweaker={false} vs sidebarTone=\"white\" tweaker={false}; dataset=\"northside-dental\" tweaker={false} pins the client.\n- ProposalSidebar renders the proposal IA by default; pass `sections` only when a screen needs a different nav. Nav rows support `goto: \"<screen name>\"` and `transition` fields — flows live in the DATA, not in markup.\n- ACTIVE NAV ROW: every screen states which page it is via activeId on the sidebar — <ProposalSidebar activeId=\"location-profile-hours\" />. NAV MODEL v2: top-level rows are page LINKS (no accordions); a section's sub rows appear ONLY while the viewer is inside that section (activeId on it or a descendant). Subs are max ONE level — level-3 ids highlight their level-2 parent and level-3 content belongs ON the page (header tabs), not in the nav. Row ids are the section/sub ids in PROPOSAL_SECTIONS (e.g. \"rankings-table\", \"location-profile-hours\", \"reviews\"). Always set activeId on a screen that represents a specific page.\n- TOP-LEVEL LANDINGS: every top-level nav item has a landing screen named exactly like its label (\"AI Insights\", \"Rankings\", \"All Locations\", ... — tagged section:\"Top-Level Pages\" in the share project), and the module's DEFAULT navLinks already wire every top-level row (and rankings-table) to them. Do NOT re-wire top-level navigation per screen; per-screen navLinks are for OVERRIDES and deep links only. Breadcrumb root is \"All Locations\" (goto: \"All Locations\"), then \"Location Hub\" for location-scoped pages.\n- NAV GOTO LINKS are per-project DATA, not structure: data.navLinks maps row id → goto target (or { goto, transition }) and is applied to the default nav automatically. Wire flows by putting navLinks in the project's ProposalDataProvider data or dataset JSON rather than forking sections.\n- GOTO BY ID (DEFAULT): a goto target should be \"screen:<id>\" — ids pin the exact screen and SURVIVE RENAMES; a bare screen name still resolves (case-insensitive) but breaks the day the screen is renamed, so names are only for hand-authoring when the id isn't known yet. When wiring by id, put the screen NAME in a trailing comment for readability (see the module's DEFAULT navLinks). Screen ids come from the Studio screens list / share URLs.\n- HubStatCard / HubHeroCard accept `goto` / `transition` props for screen-to-screen links (they stamp data-grade-goto / data-grade-transition).\n- LOCATION CARDS: <LocationCard location={l} goto=\"screen:dmrurue2wmp9u\" /> renders one entry from data.locations. DATASET CARRY (snag 4 fix): entries may carry `dataset: \"<name>\"` — clicking the card stashes it session-wide (selectSessionDataset) BEFORE the goto, so the destination hub and every screen after render THE CLICKED location. Priority in AppLayoutShell: tweaker Data row > session-selected > authored dataset prop. It (photo with No-photo placeholder, name, MapPin/Tag/Phone rows — missing fields self-skip; `status` renders as a Badge pill ON the photo top-right, live-site style — Active → variant=\"primary\" (green success), anything else → \"outline\"). `loading` renders <LocationCardSkeleton /> (also exported) with the same footprint. Defaults to the DS Card's density=\"condensed\" (the roomy default read bloated in a grid); pass density=\"default\" to override. Individual props (name/city/postcode/category/phone/photo) override the location object. The All Locations page is a grid of these; don't hand-roll location tiles.\n- CARD GAP IS 16px EVERYWHERE CARDS MEET: grids of Cards (LocationCard, module cards, stat cards) use `gap-4`, and STACKED cards/sections use `gap-4` ON GlobalLayoutContentBody — the DS body is ALREADY `flex flex-col gap-6`, so pass `className=\"gap-4\"` (tailwind-merge collapses it); `space-y-*` there ADDS margins on top of the flex gap (the invisible ~40px bug — Ali, 23 Jul). Reserve `gap-6` for layout grids INSIDE a single Card and for toolbar→content rhythm; never for card-to-card spacing on any axis.\n- BREADCRUMB DATA BINDING: a crumb can be `{ bind: \"location\", goto: \"…\" }` — PageHeader resolves the label from data.location.name at render, so location-scoped pages show the REAL location name in the trail. Location-scoped landings use [{ label: \"All Locations\", goto: \"All Locations\" }, { bind: \"location\", goto: \"Location Hub - New Template\" }].\n- DATASET PROVENANCE: each lib/data/*.json carries a `provenance` map — dotted data paths → \"brightlocal-source\" (captured from the live product) | \"proposed\" (our enrichment/summarising). minus-one-studios mixes both (real scores/insights; proposed ledes, action labels, summaries, subMetrics); harbour-co and northside-dental are wholly proposed (\"*\"). Consult it before treating a number as real; mark new fields as you add them.\n- ALL LOCATIONS LOOPS THE DATASETS: data.locations = datasetLocations() (one entry per lib/data/*.json — name/city/postcode/category/photo/phone from the dataset's `location` block, `dataset` = filename) + the legacy dirty-data entries. Adding a dataset file ADDS a clickable location; never hand-add a dataset-backed location.\n- NAP DISCIPLINE: data.location is { name, address, phone, status } — `name` is the business NAME ONLY, never name-plus-address. Breadcrumbs bind the bare name; PageHeader's default meta composes `name — address` for display. Same rule for entries in data.locations. Never concatenate address or phone into a name field.\n- Data comes from the proposal data seam: `useProposalData()` returns { account, user, location, keywords, aiInsights, foundation.*, visibility, competitors, metrics.* } with demo defaults; wrap a screen in <ProposalDataProvider data={{…partial…}} /> or name a dataset (<ProposalDataProvider dataset=\"harbour-co\">) to re-skin the whole interface. NAMED DATASETS are raw JSON at registries/brightlocal/lib/data/<name>.json — partial patches deep-merged over the defaults (arrays replace wholesale). Merge order: defaults → dataset → data prop. Datasets: \"harbour-co\" (healthy scores), \"northside-dental\" (weak scores), \"minus-one-studios\" (REAL numbers captured from the live product's Location Summary page). Every dataset's location.status is \"Active\" — the only status VERIFIED on live; don't invent others. ASSUMPTION (Ali to verify): the sidebar trial line (\"Trial: 14 days left\") was restored on minus-one-studios ONLY (it matches Ali's real trial account); harbour-co/northside-dental keep their invented plan personas (\"Pro — annual\"/\"Agency plan\") — flip those to the trial line too if every demo should read as a trial.\n- PREFER DATA BINDING over hardcoded values: HubStatCard takes metricKey=\"reviews\" (reads metric/delta/description from data.metrics.reviews); PageHeader's default meta is the current location; ProposalSidebar's Local Search Grid keyword rows come from data.keywords. Explicit props always win over bound values.\n- SHAPE CONVENTION: each area of the product (rankings, reviews, citations, AI insights, …) gets its OWN top-level section in the data JSON — tables, keyword lists, insight items live under their area key, and screens bind with useProposalData().<area>. The data shape is SEPARATE from the nav structure — SECTIONS stay in the module; data never defines nav depth.\n- AI INSIGHTS SHAPE (matched to the live product): aiInsights is { lastUpdated, updatesLeft, counts: { insights, recommendations }, tone, summaries: { concise|standard|detailed }, atAGlance: [3 strings], items: [{ id, area, areaLabel, severity, title, insight, recommendation, action, actions: [{ text, links?: [{ label, area? }] }] }] }. TEXT VARIES, NUMBERS DON'T: bind aiInsights.summary (the provider resolves summaries[tone]); switch voice with a one-key patch data={{ aiInsights: { tone: \"concise\" } }}; author text in summaries.<tone>, never in summary. areaLabel is the OLD-platform sidebar name (\"Web Performance\" ↔ \"Website and content\"); area is the nav row for drill-through; deep links live per-ACTION (a card's single CTA = first link across its actions). Screens compose the keys freely: hero = summary; compact card = title + action; full page = insight + recommendation + actions.\n- LOCATION SCORE IS ARITHMETIC, NEVER AUTHORED: computeLocationScore(data) → { overall, foundation, visibility } from scoreModel weights (overall 50/50; foundation = Website 30 / GBP 30 / Reviews 20 / Citations 20; visibility = Maps 60 / Organic 40). The only authored numbers are foundation.<module>.score (+ subMetrics) and visibility.googleMaps/organic.score.\n- HUB-AND-SPOKE (progressive disclosure): foundation.<module> cards on a summary/hub screen show the topline score and drill through their `area` via navLinks to the module's own page (Reviews, Citations, …); subMetrics bars belong on the drill-down page (or a deliberately dense management variant), not the hub.\n- DON'T STARVE THE PAGE: the seam covers shared identity/area data, not every value on screen. For page-specific content the seam doesn't carry (table rows, chart series, one-off lists), invent rich realistic in-page data as usual — in-page constants are fine and explicit props always beat bound values. Never render a sparse or empty page because useProposalData() lacks a key; bind what exists, invent the rest.\n- AppLayoutShell also takes dataset=\"<name>\" (authored) and the ShellTweakerPanel has a Data row — Alt+T switches datasets live, session-only.\n- Do NOT re-implement or inline copies of AppLayoutShell, ProposalSidebar, PageHeader, HubStatCard, HubHeroCard, or ShellTweakerPanel in a screen — the module is the single source of truth; screens that import it update automatically when it changes.\n\nCopy voice for all generated text:\n- Plain English at roughly a 9-year-old reading age: short sentences, common words, active voice.\n- Buttons start with a verb and match product casing (\"Run New Audit\", \"Add Location\", \"Build Citations\").\n- Numbers as digits; dates as \"13 Jul 2026\"; percentages with the % sign.\n- No exclamation marks and no marketing superlatives inside product UI.\n- Empty states: one sentence saying what will appear here, plus the primary action.\n- Errors: say what happened and how to fix it; never blame the user.\n- Explain jargon on first use (see glossary); never leave an unexplained acronym in user-facing copy.\n\nBrightLocal DS audit log — known gaps between their docs, the npm package (2.20.0), and the live platform. Work around these; don't fight them. (This file doubles as the running findings ledger for the upstream report — append new discrepancies here.)\n- --sidebar-width is set INLINE by SidebarProvider (224px) and hardcoded on the live platform's container — not overridable from :root. The project's custom.css patches it at [data-slot=\"sidebar-provider\"]; never try to set it via classes or :root vars.\n- Sidebar icon sizing: AI_USAGE says 16px-no-overrides, the sidebar docs say nothing, the live platform ships 24px at strokeWidth 1.33. Our convention is 20px size-5 (see the product map) — follow it, not the live platform.\n- Account dropdown: the live platform wraps the user in a bordered card (data-slot=\"dropdown-menu-trigger-avatar\") that does NOT exist in the published package; Storybook shows only a bare logout. Use SidebarAccountDropdown exactly as published — do not fake the border with utility classes.\n- Mobile shell: SidebarTrigger + the built-in mobile sheet exist in the package but are documented nowhere — mount them per the product map's responsive-shell rule; never hand-roll a drawer.\n- component-meta.json lists ~23 exports that don't exist in the real barrel and omits SidebarAccountDropdown entirely — trust the component allowlist, not component-meta.\n- data-hook names INSTANCES (\"settings-save-button\"), not components; component identity comes from data-slot.\n- Their MCP composition recipes appear nowhere in Storybook, and 8 of 29 contain placeholder slots that have never been rendered — treat recipe slots as structure, not finished content.\n- The LIVE platform's display font doesn't render: headings declare Poppins (inspector shows the stack) but paint in a fallback — the double-storey 'a' gives it away (Poppins' 'a' is single-storey). Likely a failed webfont load masked by font-display: swap. Studio's preview loads real Poppins from Google Fonts, so generated screens are MORE on-brand than production — when comparing against live screenshots, trust the DS tokens (--ds-font-font-display: Poppins), not live's rendered type.\n- Card ships max-w-[400px] in its BASE classes — any card meant to span a slot wider than 400px silently clamps (\"why is my card narrow?\"). Their own deprecation message sanctions the fix: className max-width utilities. Add className=\"max-w-none\" to full-width cards (and w-full where the card must stretch). The old maxWidth prop is deprecated — never use it.\n- Badge has NO success/warning/info variants (their MCP lists this as a limitation) — yet the live product's \"Active\" status badge paints itself with className=\"border-transparent bg-success-background text-success-foreground\", violating their own no-restyling rule out of necessity. This exact className is the ONE sanctioned exception for status badges until they ship the variants (see the location-page-header recipe).\n- Icon stroke invariant (undocumented): the live platform keeps the ABSOLUTE stroke at ~1.33px across sizes — 24px icons at strokeWidth 1.33, 14px meta icons at 2.28 (both ≈ 1.33px drawn). A real system, written down nowhere.\n- The published npm package lags the live product (account trigger, possibly more). When a live screenshot disagrees with what the package renders, the package wins for generation; log the difference here.\n- GlobalLayout bakes `p-section-sm` into its inner div as a STRING LITERAL (no cn() merge — the className prop lands on the outer ScrollArea), and ScrollArea hardcodes `p-1` on data-slot=\"scroll-area-viewport\" (focus-ring breathing room; className only reaches the Root). Neither is prop-overridable — and the live platform DOES ship this padding too (verified July 2026), so this is a proposal-level deviation from live, not a package/live drift. It blocks sticky headers and edge-locked layouts. The project's custom.css zeroes both, scoped: `[data-hook=\"global-layout-scroll\"] > [data-slot=\"scroll-area-viewport\"]` + `[data-slot=\"global-layout\"]`. When the padding is stripped, GlobalLayoutSidebar's sticky geometry (top/height offset by --ds-section-padding-y-sm) must be reset per-screen via style={{ top: 0, height: \"100dvh\" }} (its style prop merges, user wins). Upstream ask: run the padding through cn(), or expose it as a prop/variant.\n- The desktop Sidebar paints NO background: `bg-sidebar-background` exists only in its mobile Sheet branch (2.20.0 dist); the desktop branch sets `text-sidebar-foreground` only, so the \"sidebar background\" is the page background showing through — invisible because both tokens resolve to neutral-50. Re-pointing --sidebar-background recolors nothing on desktop; anything theming the sidebar area must paint the container itself (AppLayoutShell's sidebarTone sets backgroundColor + color directly, plus the --sidebar-*/--color-sidebar-* doubles for the accent/hover utilities). Upstream ask: paint bg-sidebar-background on the desktop branch too, so the token actually themes the sidebar.\n- Sidebar dividers: use the sidebar family's OWN `SidebarSeparator` (bg-sidebar-border, tone-aware), NOT the generic `Separator` — that one paints the PAGE border token (`bg-border`), which reads harsh inside a toned sidebar. SidebarSeparator's only flaw is its baked `my-5` rhythm (20px each side, airier than the tightened nav); override with className=\"my-2\" (their cn is tailwind-merge, so the override wins). Upstream ask: give SidebarSeparator a spacing variant like Separator's, and have the Sidebar docs point at it — nothing currently steers composition away from the generic Separator.\n- Nav truncation is BAKED IN: SidebarMenuButton and SidebarMenuSubButton hardcode `[&>span:last-of-type]:truncate`, and SidebarMenuSub's border variant bakes `pr-10` (40px right padding per nesting level) — together they force ellipsis on deep/long nav labels with no opt-out. Navigation labels must never truncate (data values like business names may). Workaround: `[&>span:last-of-type]:whitespace-normal!` + h-auto/min-h rows + overriding pr-10 via className. Upstream ask: make truncation opt-in and the sub-list right padding a variant/token.\n\n## Chip vs Badge (16 Jul 2026)\n\n`Chip` ALWAYS renders a remove button (`data-slot=\"chip-delete\"`; a loading spinner replaces it while `loading`) — there is no non-dismissible mode in 2.20.0. It is an INPUT control (filters, selected values), never a status label. For read-only status, plan, or delta labels use `Badge` (primary | secondary | destructive | outline). Symptom that prompted this: every \"badge\" on the hub cards sprouting a ✕.\n\n## SidebarContent overflow nudge (16 Jul 2026)\n\n`SidebarContent` adds `pr-2` only when its nav overflows (`hasOverflow` state in sidebar.tsx), so the entire nav shifts 8px left the moment it becomes scrollable. Account for it by passing `pr-2` permanently via className — cn dedupes when the DS adds its own. Upstream ask: reserve the gutter unconditionally (or scrollbar-gutter: stable).\n\n## Font-weight ramp is shifted (16 Jul 2026)\n\nThe theme remaps the whole weight scale one step down: `--ds-font-weight-normal: 300`, `medium: 400`, `semibold: 500`, `bold: 600`. Consequence: DS defaults like CardDescription's `font-normal` paint at 300 — visibly light/thin, especially in Inter at text-sm. For body copy that should read as regular weight, use `font-medium` (= 400). Deliberate at token level, but worth an upstream conversation about whether description text should sit at 300. PROPOSAL DECISION (same day): every proposal screen re-points the ramp to the standard scale (normal=400 … bold=700) via BRIGHTLOCAL_WEIGHT_RAMP_FIX appended to the registry previewThemeCss — weight utilities mean what they say, no per-component overrides needed. The upstream recommendation is to ship that ramp.\n\n## Card border token is transparent (16 Jul 2026)\n\n`--ds-colors-card-border-light` maps to `base-transparent` — the filled Card's `border border-card-border` renders NO visible border by default. Figma's card border is `base/border` (= neutral-200, #E6EDE8), i.e. the semantic `--border` token, not `--card-border`. Proposal screens paint `border-color: var(--border)` on cards via the raised layer. Upstream ask: point card-border at base/border (or document the transparent default).";
